---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Related from '../components/Related.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE, SOURCES } from '../lib/editorial.js';

const title = 'Service Charge on a Restaurant Bill: Should You Tip Again?';
const description =
  'Learn how to read automatic gratuities, service fees, hospitality fees and unclear restaurant charges without guessing from the percentage alone.';

const nav = [
  { href: '#guide', label: 'Guide' },
  { href: '#tax-treatment', label: 'IRS distinction' },
  { href: '#faq', label: 'FAQ' },
  { href: '#sources', label: 'Sources' },
];

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  '@id': 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/#breadcrumb',
  itemListElement: [
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Restaurant Tip Calculator',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Service Charge on Restaurant Bill',
      item: 'https://restauranttipcalculator.com/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/',
    },
  ],
});
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/" jsonLd={jsonLd} nav={nav}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="guide-intro"><a href="/">Restaurant tip calculator</a> / Service charges explained</p>
    <h1>Service Charge on a Restaurant Bill: Should You Tip Again?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>Read the label and the restaurant's disclosure before adding another full tip.</strong> If the check
      clearly says an automatic gratuity has already been added, many diners treat that as the gratuity for the meal
      and add more only if they want to. A service, hospitality, wellness, or administrative fee does not tell you by
      itself whether it replaces a gratuity or how the restaurant uses the money.
    </p>
    <p class="menu-src">
      This guide covers U.S. tipping and federal tax context unless otherwise noted. Research last reviewed:
      {RESEARCH_REVIEW_DATE}. <a href="/methodology/">See our methodology.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="guide">
    <h2>How to read a charge before deciding whether to tip again</h2>
    <p class="guide-intro">
      The percentage is not a reliable shortcut. A 5%, 18%, or 20% charge can mean different things at different
      restaurants. Use the wording and disclosure instead.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="worked worked-wide">
      <caption>Restaurant charge decision framework</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What the check says</th>
          <th scope="col">What you can safely infer</th>
          <th scope="col">Practical next step</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Automatic gratuity / mandatory gratuity</td>
          <td>A mandatory gratuity amount has already been added.</td>
          <td>Do not add a second full gratuity automatically. Any extra amount is voluntary.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Service / hospitality / wellness / administrative fee</td>
          <td>The restaurant has added a charge, but the label alone does not show who receives it or whether extra tipping is expected.</td>
          <td>Read the menu or check disclosure. Ask the restaurant how the fee works if the disclosure is unclear.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Unclear charge</td>
          <td>The percentage alone does not identify it as a gratuity.</td>
          <td>Ask before adding another full tip so you do not double-pay by mistake.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Do not use a percentage rule</h3>
      <p>
        A small fee is not automatically "just overhead," and a charge near 18–20% is not automatically the server's
        gratuity. Restaurant policies vary, so this site does not use fee-size thresholds to infer whether a charge
        replaces a gratuity.
      </p>

      <h3>What if the check has both a charge and a tip line?</h3>
      <p>
        A blank tip line does not, by itself, explain an existing charge. Check the restaurant's wording first. If an
        automatic gratuity is clearly disclosed, an additional tip can still be left voluntarily. If the existing
        line is only described as a service or hospitality fee, ask how the restaurant treats it before deciding what
        to add.
      </p>

      <h3>Can you refuse a restaurant service charge?</h3>
      <p>
        There is no useful nationwide yes-or-no answer here. Disclosure and consumer rules can vary by jurisdiction
        and circumstance. If a charge was unexpected, review the menu or booking disclosure and raise it with restaurant
        management. This page provides general information, not legal advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="tax-treatment">
    <h2>What the IRS distinction actually means</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        For <strong>federal tax purposes</strong>, the IRS distinguishes voluntary tips from mandatory service charges.
        A payment is a tip when the customer voluntarily decides to pay it and determines the amount. Mandatory
        charges, including automatic gratuities, are service charges rather than tips for this classification.
      </p>
      <p>
        The IRS also says a service charge that an employer distributes to an employee is generally treated as wages.
        That tax rule does <strong>not</strong> tell a diner that every restaurant distributes a charge the same way,
        nor does it tell you whether that restaurant expects an additional gratuity. Those are separate questions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Source: <a href={SOURCES.irsTips}>IRS — Tip recordkeeping and reporting</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="worked worked-wide">
      <caption>Simple arithmetic examples on a $100 meal, before tax</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Bill detail</th><th scope="col">Amount due before any extra voluntary tip</th><th scope="col">What the example does not assume</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$100 meal + $20 automatic gratuity</td><td>$120</td><td>Whether you choose to add anything extra</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$100 meal + $5 service fee</td><td>$105</td><td>Whether the fee replaces a gratuity or who receives it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="worked-note">
      Once you know which amounts belong on your bill, the <a href="/">restaurant tip calculator</a> can include tax
      and other fees without treating those fees as part of the percentage tip basis.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="faq">
    <h2>Service charge questions, answered</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>Should I tip if an automatic gratuity is already included?</summary>
        <p>
          If the bill clearly identifies a mandatory automatic gratuity, you do not need to add a second full
          gratuity automatically. You can still add an extra voluntary tip if you want to.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does a service fee go to the server?</summary>
        <p>
          The label alone does not answer that. Restaurant policies differ. Read the restaurant's disclosure or ask
          how the charge is used before assuming it either replaces a tip or goes entirely to the server.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is an automatic gratuity a tip for tax purposes?</summary>
        <p>
          The IRS treats mandatory charges such as automatic gratuities as service charges rather than voluntary tips
          for federal tax purposes. If distributed to an employee, the amount is generally treated as wages.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does an 18% or 20% service charge mean I am done tipping?</summary>
        <p>
          Not necessarily. The percentage does not identify the charge. If it is explicitly an automatic gratuity,
          that is meaningful; if it is a generic service or hospitality fee, read the disclosure or ask the restaurant.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>
        <a href={SOURCES.irsTips}>Internal Revenue Service — Tip recordkeeping and reporting</a>
        — federal distinction between voluntary tips and mandatory service charges; reviewed September 2026.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="menu-src">
      Restaurant policies and local rules can change. <a href="/methodology/">Read how this site separates legal/tax facts from practical guidance.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot format="native" position="inline" id="ad-native" />

  <Related exclude="/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/" />
</Layout>
